section_slug,section_title,condition,evaluation_data,disposition,condition_html,evaluation_data_html,disposition_html
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,A. AME determines the individual is NOT at increased risk for sudden or subtle incapacitation.,"When a diagnosis or condition is not listed in the disposition tables, the AME should assess whether the condition or its treatment could lead to sudden or subtle incapacitation. After reviewing the condition and submitted reports, document the rationale for issuance in Item 60 and upload supporting documentation. The FAA may request additional evaluation data. If uncertain, the AME may wish to contact AMCD or RFS and discuss.",ISSUE and explain rationale in Item 60.,"<p>A. AME determines the individual is</p>
<p><strong>NOT at increased risk </strong></p>
<p>for sudden or subtle incapacitation.</p>","<p>When a diagnosis or condition is not listed in the disposition tables, the AME should assess whether the condition or its treatment could lead to sudden or subtle incapacitation.</p>
<p>After reviewing the condition and submitted reports, document the rationale for issuance in Item 60 and upload supporting documentation. The FAA may request additional evaluation data.</p>
<p>If uncertain, the AME may wish to contact AMCD or RFS and discuss.</p>","<p><strong>ISSUE</strong></p>
<p>and <strong>explain rationale</strong> in Item 60.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,B. AME determines the individual IS at increased risk for sudden or subtle incapacitation.,"Medical certificates must not be issued to an individual with medical condition(s) that requires deferral, or for any condition not listed in the table that may result in sudden or subtle incapacitation without consulting the AMCD or the RFS. To help expedite the review process, submit the following to the FAA for review: - Most recent detailed Clinical Progress Note(s) - Results of recent testing already performed pertinent to the condition - Any related treatment records After reviewing this documentation, the FAA may request additional evaluation data.",DEFER Upload copies of any documentation/reports to the FAA for review. Explain in Item 60 what aspect or concern caused the deferral.,"<p>B. AME determines the individual</p>
<p><strong>IS at increased risk </strong></p>
<p>for sudden or subtle incapacitation.</p>","<p>Medical certificates must not be issued to an individual with medical condition(s) that requires deferral, or for any condition not listed in the table that may result in sudden or subtle incapacitation without consulting the AMCD or the RFS.</p>
<p>To help expedite the review process, submit the following to the FAA for review:</p>
<ul><li>Most recent detailed Clinical Progress Note(s)</li><li>Results of recent testing already performed pertinent to the condition</li><li>Any related treatment records</li></ul>
<p>After reviewing this documentation, the FAA may request additional evaluation data.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER</strong></p>
<p>Upload copies of any documentation/reports to the FAA for review.</p>
<p>Explain in Item 60 what aspect or concern caused the deferral.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,A. Allergies controlled by acceptable medications (single or in combination) Allergy shots/Sublingual Immunotherapy (SLIT) Epi pen is prescribed,"The AME should inquire if the applicant has ever experienced any barotitis (‘ear block’), barosinusitis (‘sinus block’), alternobaric vertigo (‘dizziness’), difficulty breathing, rashes, or any other localized or systemic symptoms that could interfere with aviation safety. If the AME can determine: - The condition is under control; - Current medications are acceptable; - See the Pharmaceuticals Section, Allergy - Antihistamine & Immunotherapy Medication. - Any no fly time is discussed with the individual; AND - No symptoms that would interfere with flight or safety related duties:","ISSUE Annotate this in Block 60. Warn for any required ""no fly"" time for conditionally acceptable (Sedating) Antihistamine Medications. If not addressed in the progress note or AME notes, the individual may be asked to provide information to the FAA.","<p><strong>A. </strong>Allergies controlled by acceptable medications (single or in combination)</p>
<p>Allergy shots/Sublingual Immunotherapy (SLIT)</p>
<p>Epi pen is prescribed</p>","<p>The AME should inquire if the applicant has ever experienced any barotitis (‘ear block’), barosinusitis (‘sinus block’), alternobaric vertigo (‘dizziness’), difficulty breathing, rashes, or any other localized or systemic symptoms that could interfere with aviation safety. If the AME can determine:</p>
<ul><li>The condition is under control;</li><li>Current medications are acceptable;<ul><li>See the <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/about/office_org/headquarters_offices/avs/offices/aam/ame/guide/media/AllergyAntihistamineImmunotherapyMedication.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">Pharmaceuticals Section, Allergy - Antihistamine &amp; Immunotherapy Medication.</a></li></ul></li><li>Any no fly time is discussed with the individual; AND</li><li>No symptoms that would interfere with flight or safety related duties:</li></ul>","<p>ISSUE</p>
<p><strong>Annotate</strong> this in Block 60. Warn for any required &quot;no fly&quot; time for conditionally acceptable (Sedating) Antihistamine Medications.</p>
<p>If not addressed in the progress note or AME notes, the individual may be asked to provide information to the FAA.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,B. Allergies indicated by; - Chronic or repeated steroid treatment or - Reaction requiring urgent medical intervention - airway closure - urticaria requiring treatment,"Submit the following for FAA review: A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating allergist, ENT, or pulmonologist no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; results of any testing performed; - diagnosis; - assessment and plan; - prognosis; and follow-up.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.,"<p><strong>B. </strong>Allergies indicated by;</p>
<ul><li>Chronic or repeated steroid treatment or</li><li>Reaction requiring urgent medical intervention</li><li>airway closure</li><li>urticaria requiring treatment</li></ul>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p>A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>allergist, ENT, or pulmonologist </strong>no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include:</p>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings; results of any testing performed;</li><li>diagnosis;</li><li>assessment and plan;</li><li>prognosis; and follow-up.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,C. History of anaphylaxis or allergic reactions requiring use of epi-pen,"After using an epi-pen or similar, the individual should follow up with their treating physician before returning to flight or safety related duties. Submit the following for FAA review: A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating allergist, ENT, or pulmonologist. It should verify the cause of the anaphylaxis or allergic reaction, likelihood it will re-occur and if it were to re-occur, how severe.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.,<p><strong>C</strong>. History of anaphylaxis or allergic reactions requiring use of epi-pen</p>,"<p>After using an epi-pen or similar, the individual should follow up with their treating physician before returning to flight or safety related duties. Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p>A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>allergist, ENT, or pulmonologist. It </strong></p>
<p><strong>should verify the cause of the anaphylaxis or allergic reaction, likelihood it will re-occur and if it were to re-occur, how severe. </strong></p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,"- A. KNOWN etiology Including COVID-19 infection If due to trauma associated with traumatic brain injury, tumor removal, etc., review that section for additional information or required recovery periods.","No evaluations or follow-up needed if the AME can determine the condition is benign and the pilot has no other condition(s) that would interfere with flight duties: ***************************************** *** Discuss with the pilot: - This condition may cause an inability to receive early warning of fuel leaks, exhaust fumes, or a fire (prior to visible smoke). - Importance of using of a carbon monoxide (CO) detector (not a spot detector) in all aircraft flown that have an internal combustion engine. - CO is odorless and tasteless; however, it is frequently accompanied by exhaust fumes that can be detected by smell. Encourage the pilot to ask someone else to verify the absence of fuel fumes in the cabin prior to flight.","ISSUE Annotate this information in Block 60. For any identified underling condition(s), see that section.","<ul><li><strong>A. KNOWN etiology </strong></li></ul>
<p>Including <strong>COVID-19 infection</strong></p>
<p>If due to trauma associated with traumatic brain injury, tumor removal, etc., review that section for additional information or required recovery periods.</p>","<p>No evaluations or follow-up needed if the AME can determine the condition is benign and the pilot has no other condition(s) that would interfere with flight duties:</p>
<p>***************************************** ***</p>
<p>Discuss with the pilot:</p>
<ul><li>This condition may cause an inability to receive early warning of <strong>fuel </strong>leaks, exhaust fumes, or a <strong>fire</strong> (prior to visible smoke).</li><li>Importance of using of a carbon monoxide (CO) detector (not a spot detector) in all aircraft flown that have an internal combustion engine.</li><li>CO is odorless and tasteless; however, it is frequently accompanied by exhaust fumes that can be detected by smell. Encourage the pilot to ask someone else to verify the absence of fuel fumes in the cabin prior to flight.</li></ul>","<p><strong>ISSUE </strong></p>
<p>Annotate this information in Block 60. For any identified underling condition(s), see that section.</p>"
condition-not-listed,Condition Not Listed,- B. UNKNOWN (or uncertain) etiology For ANY duration.,"Submit the following to the FAA for review: - The most recent detailed Clinical Progress Note (actual clinical record) from an otolaryngologist (ENT). - It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis, current medications, clinical exam findings, results of any testing performed, diagnosis, assessment, plan (prognosis), and follow-up. - It must specifically include etiology, if found.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Follow up Issuance will be per the airman’s authorization letter.,"<ul><li><strong>B. UNKNOWN </strong>(or uncertain)</li></ul>
<p><strong>etiology </strong></p>
<p>For <strong>ANY</strong> duration.</p>","<p>Submit the following to the FAA for review:</p>
<ul><li>The most recent <strong>detailed Clinical Progress Note</strong> (actual clinical record) from an <strong>otolaryngologist (ENT)</strong>.</li><li>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis, current medications, clinical exam findings, results of any testing performed, diagnosis, assessment, plan (prognosis), and follow-up.</li><li>It must specifically include etiology, if found.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p><strong>Follow up Issuance </strong>will be per the airman’s authorization letter.</p>"
item-27-sinuses,ITEM 27. Sinuses,"A. Acute infection(s) from any cause requiring medication(s) or not OR Deviated nasal septum (not causing obstruction) corrected or not Note: Recurrent, isolated infections which clear can be followed under Row A.","If the AME can determine the condition has resolved without sequelae with no symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties: Notes: If the airman is currently on antibiotics or symptomatic, the treatment course should be completed before medical certification can be considered. Verify medications listed are acceptable. 61.53 applies. Pilots should not fly with a “cold” or viral upper respiratory tract infection, or with the condition of rhinosinusitis.",ISSUE Annotate this information in Block 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong></p>
<p>Acute infection(s) from any cause requiring medication(s) or not</p>
<p>OR</p>
<p>Deviated nasal septum (not causing obstruction) corrected or not</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Recurrent, isolated infections which clear can be followed under Row A.</p>","<p>If the AME can determine the condition has resolved without sequelae with no symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties:</p>
<p><strong>Notes: </strong>If the airman is currently on antibiotics or symptomatic, the treatment course should be completed before medical certification can be considered.</p>
<p>Verify medications listed are acceptable.</p>
<p>61.53 applies.</p>
<p>Pilots should not fly with a “cold” or viral upper respiratory tract infection, or with the condition of rhinosinusitis.</p>","<p>ISSUE</p>
<p><strong>Annotate</strong> this information in</p>
<p>Block 60.</p>"
item-27-sinuses,ITEM 27. Sinuses,"B. Chronic or severe (hospitalized for infection) OR Affected by barometric changes OR Sinus Obstruction from nasal polyps or cysts, tumor, anatomic (deviated nasal septum)","If the AME history and physical exam confirm the condition is chronic, severe, or symptomatic, the airman should not fly until resolved. Submit the following for FAA review: 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating ENT (preferred) or allergist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition. - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment and plan (prognosis); and - Follow-up. 2. Personal statement discussing symptoms, severity, how often they occur, and treatment used, any prior surgery or trauma. 3. Sinus CT imaging (report) already performed; 4. Results of evaluation(s) for underlying conditions such as polyps, ciliary dysmotility syndrome, autoimmune disease, immune suppression, or anatomic abnormalities; Pilots should NOT fly with a ""cold"" or viral upper respiratory tract infection, or with the condition of rhinosinusitis.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.,"<p>B.<strong> Chronic</strong> or severe (hospitalized for infection)</p>
<p>OR</p>
<p>Affected by barometric changes</p>
<p>OR</p>
<p>Sinus Obstruction</p>
<p>from nasal polyps or cysts, tumor, anatomic (deviated nasal septum)</p>","<p>If the AME history and physical exam confirm the condition is chronic, severe, or symptomatic, the airman should not fly until resolved.</p>
<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p><strong>1.</strong> A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating ENT (preferred) or allergist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It must include:</p>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition.</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment and plan (prognosis); and</li><li>Follow-up.</li></ul>
<p><strong>2.</strong> Personal statement discussing symptoms, severity, how often they occur, and treatment used, any prior surgery or trauma.</p>
<p><strong>3.</strong> Sinus CT imaging (report) already performed;</p>
<p><strong>4. </strong> Results of evaluation(s) for underlying conditions such as polyps, ciliary dysmotility syndrome, autoimmune disease, immune suppression, or anatomic abnormalities;</p>
<p>Pilots should NOT fly with a &quot;cold&quot; or viral upper respiratory tract infection, or with the condition of rhinosinusitis.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>"
item-28-mouth-and-throat,ITEM 28. Mouth and Throat,A. Resolved OR Does not impair voice communication.,If the AME can determine the condition has resolved without sequelae with no symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties: - Communication needed for flight (emergency procedures; - Effective communication with air traffic control; and - Crew-cockpit communication).,ISSUE Annotate this information in Block 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong></p>
<p>Resolved</p>
<p>OR</p>
<p>Does not impair voice communication.</p>","<p>If the AME can determine the condition has resolved without sequelae with no symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties:</p>
<ul><li>Communication needed for flight (emergency procedures;</li><li>Effective communication with air traffic control; and</li><li>Crew-cockpit communication).</li></ul>","<p>ISSUE</p>
<p><strong>Annotate</strong> this information in</p>
<p>Block 60.</p>"
item-28-mouth-and-throat,ITEM 28. Mouth and Throat,"B. Condition, including stuttering, that would impair voice communication","Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with a speech-language pathologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It must include: - Detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment; - Plan (prognosis); and - Follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include the cause for stuttering and the intelligible their speech is. - Stuttering Severity Index for those who stutter; - Speech Fluency/Intelligibility test for those with mechanical problems; - 3. Any testing deemed necessary or already performed by the treating provider. The speech-language pathologist or AME should describe any functional limitations that could affect the pilot's ability to operate aircraft controls or communicate with other pilots or ATCS.",DEFER AME should comment on how well they can communicate. Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.,"<p><strong>B. </strong></p>
<p>Condition, including stuttering, that would impair voice communication</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li>1. A current, detailed <strong>Clinical Progress Note</strong> generated from a clinic visit with a <strong>speech-language pathologist</strong> no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It must include:</li><li>Detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment;</li><li>Plan (prognosis); and</li><li>Follow-up.</li><li>2. <strong>It must specifically include </strong>the cause for stuttering and the intelligible their speech is.<ul><li><strong>Stuttering Severity Index for those who stutter;</strong></li><li><strong>Speech Fluency/Intelligibility test for those with mechanical problems;</strong></li></ul></li><li>3. Any testing deemed necessary or already performed by the treating provider.</li></ul>
<p>The speech-language pathologist or AME should describe any functional limitations that could affect the pilot&#x27;s ability to operate aircraft controls or communicate with other pilots or ATCS.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p><strong> AME should comment on how well they can communicate.</strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",A. If the CI was placed in an adult to improve speech discrimination and sound localization (usually seen in older adults) OR Single Sided Deafness (SSD) Note: Pilots with a CI should fly with a spare processor and extra batteries.,"If the AME can determine: - The individual can pass the hearing requirements with the CI turned OFF (i.e., SSD which passes hearing with the other ear); and - No symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties: (If unable to pass hearing tests with CI turned off, go to Row B.)",ISSUE Annotate this information in Block 60 AND List the age the CI was placed and reason. Specify if they passed the hearing test with the CI turned on or off. This does NOT require a hearing limitation or MFT.,"<p><strong>A. </strong>If the CI was placed in an adult to improve speech discrimination and sound localization (usually seen in older adults)</p>
<p>OR</p>
<p>Single Sided Deafness (SSD)</p>
<p>Note: Pilots with a CI should fly with a spare processor and extra batteries.</p>","<p>If the AME can determine:</p>
<ul><li>The individual can pass the hearing requirements with the CI turned OFF (i.e., SSD which passes hearing with the other ear); and</li><li>No symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties:</li></ul>
<p>(If unable to pass hearing tests with CI turned off, go to Row B.)</p>","<p><strong>ISSUE</strong></p>
<p><strong>Annotate</strong> this information in</p>
<p>Block 60</p>
<p>AND</p>
<p>List the age the CI was placed and reason. Specify if they passed the hearing test with the CI turned on or off.</p>
<p>This does NOT require a hearing limitation</p>
<p>or MFT.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",B. Implant was placed for hearing loss as a child/teen Note: Pilots with a CI should fly with a spare processor and extra batteries.,"Submit the following for FAA review: 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating Ear, Nose, & Throat (ENT) or audiologist no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment and plan (prognosis); - Prognosis; and follow-up. 2. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include: - Reason for the implant; - Date of implant; - How the individual functions with the device; - Ability to hear in noisy environment; and - Word discrimination 3. Audiogram performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Even if they pass the hearing test in the AME office. Annotate elements or findings in Block 60.,"<p><strong>B. </strong>Implant was placed for hearing loss as a child/teen</p>
<p>Note: Pilots with a CI should fly with a spare processor and extra batteries.</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p>1. A <strong>current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</strong> generated from a clinic visit with the treating Ear, Nose, &amp; Throat <strong>(ENT) or audiologist no more than 90 days prior </strong>to the AME exam. It must include:</p>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment and plan (prognosis);</li><li>Prognosis; and follow-up.</li></ul>
<p>2. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include:</p>
<ul><li>Reason for the implant;</li><li>Date of implant;</li><li>How the individual functions with the device;</li><li>Ability to hear in noisy environment; and</li><li>Word discrimination</li></ul>
<p>3. Audiogram performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p><strong>Even if they pass the hearing test in the AME office. </strong></p>
<p>Annotate elements or findings in</p>
<p>Block 60.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",A. Treated 5 or more years ago With - Surgery OR - Stereotactic radiation,"The AME should review a current status report from the treating physician. If no symptoms or current problems, no ongoing treatment or surveillance needed:",ISSUE Summarize history in Block 60. Submit documents to the FAA for retention in the file.,"<p><strong>A. </strong> Treated</p>
<p><strong>5 or more years ago</strong></p>
<p>With</p>
<ul><li>Surgery OR</li><li>Stereotactic radiation</li></ul>","<p>The AME should review a current status report from the treating physician. If no symptoms or current problems, no ongoing treatment or surveillance needed:</p>","<p><strong>ISSUE </strong></p>
<p>Summarize history in Block 60.</p>
<p>Submit documents to the FAA for retention in the file.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",B. Treated 5 or more years ago With - Observation ONLY,"Submit the following to the FAA for review: - ␀ Current status report from the treating physician with treatment plan and prognosis; - It should identify all treatment used, size of the tumor at diagnosis, and current size; - ␀ List of medications and side effects, if any; - ␀ Operative notes and discharge summary, if applicable; and - ␀ Copies of most recent imaging report(s) (MRI).",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Follow up Issuance Will be per the airman’s authorization letter.,"<p><strong>B. </strong>Treated</p>
<p><strong> 5 or more years ago</strong></p>
<p>With</p>
<ul><li>Observation ONLY</li></ul>","<p>Submit the following to the FAA for review:</p>
<ul><li>␀ Current status report from the treating physician with treatment plan and prognosis;<ul><li>It should identify all treatment used, size of the tumor at diagnosis, and current size;</li></ul></li><li>␀ List of medications and side effects, if any;</li><li>␀ Operative notes and discharge summary, if applicable; and</li><li>␀ Copies of most recent imaging report(s) (MRI).</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p><strong>Follow up Issuance </strong>Will be per the airman’s authorization letter.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General","C. Treated less than 5 years ago With ANY of the following: - Observation, - Surgery, OR - Stereotactic radiation","Submit the following to the FAA for review: - ␀ Current status report from the treating physician (ENT or neurosurgeon) with - Treatment plan, prognosis, and adherence to treatment; - It should indicate the presence or absence of any residual tumor and any complications; - ␀ List of medications and side effects, if any; - ␀ Operative notes and discharge summary (if applicable); SEE NEXT PAGE - ␀ Copies of initial and most recent imaging reports (MRI) and lab; - ␀ Current audiogram (pure tone and speech discrimination); and - ␀ If any neurologic deficit is noted, current documentation of the deficit and severity, as well as the status of the rest of the neurologic exam by treating neurosurgeon or neurotologist, must be submitted.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Follow up Issuance Will be per the airman’s authorization letter.,"<p><strong>C. Treated less than 5 years ago </strong></p>
<p>With ANY of the following:</p>
<ul><li>Observation,</li><li>Surgery, OR</li><li>Stereotactic radiation</li></ul>","<p>Submit the following to the FAA for review:</p>
<ul><li>␀ Current status report from the treating physician (ENT or neurosurgeon) with</li><li>Treatment plan, prognosis, and adherence to treatment;</li><li>It should indicate the presence or absence of any residual tumor and any complications;</li><li>␀ List of medications and side effects, if any;</li><li>␀ Operative notes and discharge summary</li></ul>
<p>(if applicable); SEE NEXT PAGE</p>
<ul><li>␀ Copies of initial and most recent imaging reports (MRI) and lab;</li><li>␀ Current audiogram (pure tone and speech discrimination); and</li><li>␀ If any neurologic deficit is noted, current documentation of the deficit and severity, as well as the status of the rest of the neurologic exam by treating neurosurgeon or neurotologist, must be submitted.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p><strong>Follow up Issuance </strong>Will be per the airman’s authorization letter.</p>"
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General","Acute or chronic disease without disturbance of equilibrium and successful miringotomy, if applicable",Submit all pertinent medical information,If no physiologic effects - Issue,"<p>Acute or chronic disease without disturbance of equilibrium and successful miringotomy, if applicable</p>",<p>Submit all pertinent medical information</p>,<p>If no physiologic effects - Issue</p>
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",Acute or chronic disease that may disturb equilibrium,Submit all pertinent medical information and current status report,Requires FAA Decision,<p>Acute or chronic disease that may disturb equilibrium</p>,<p>Submit all pertinent medical information and current status report</p>,<p>Requires FAA Decision</p>
item-29-ears-general,"ITEM 29. Ears, General",A. RESOLVED AND symptoms lasted 1 year or less in total. (This may have been single or multiple episode[s]).,"If the AME can determine: - The condition has fully resolved without sequelae (with favorable notes from PCP, ER, or ENT); - No symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties; - Medications, if any, are discontinued; and - No hearing loss: Note: Symptoms should be brief, mild, not disabling, and respond to repositioning.",ISSUE Annotate this information in Block 60.,"<p><strong>A.</strong> RESOLVED</p>
<p><strong>AND </strong>symptoms lasted</p>
<p><strong>1 year or less in total. </strong></p>
<p>(This may have been single or multiple episode[s]).</p>","<p>If the AME can determine:</p>
<ul><li>The condition has fully resolved without sequelae (with favorable notes from PCP, ER, or ENT);</li><li>No symptoms or current problems that would interfere with flight duties;</li><li>Medications, if any, are discontinued; and</li><li>No hearing loss:</li></ul>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Symptoms should be brief, mild, not disabling, and respond to repositioning.</p>","<p><strong>ISSUE</strong></p>
<p><strong>Annotate</strong> this information</p>
<p>in Block 60.</p>"
