amblyopia-lazy-eye-status-summary-developmental-adaptation-to-unilateral-visual,Amblyopia (Lazy Eye) Status Summary (Developmental adaptation to unilateral visual impairment*),B. Others: - Does not meet vision standards - Quality of vision is affected - AME or ophthalmologist or optometrist have concerns,"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating ophthalmologist or optometrist no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment and plan; - Prognosis; and Follow-up. 2. The Clinical Progress note must specifically include the best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and first or second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. 3. Copies of any testing performed (visual fields, etc.) 4. Quality of Vision Questionnaire","DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60. Note: If unable to meet 1st or 2nd class visual acuity standards, but they will accept a lower class, the AME should issue 3rd class and annotate block 60.","<p><strong>B.</strong> Others:</p>
<ul><li>Does not meet vision standards</li><li>Quality of vision is affected</li><li>AME or ophthalmologist or optometrist have concerns</li></ul>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <strong>current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</strong> generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>ophthalmologist or optometrist </strong>no more than <strong>90 days</strong> prior to the AME exam. It must include:</li></ul>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment and plan;</li><li>Prognosis; and Follow-up.</li></ul>
<p>2. The Clinical Progress note must specifically include the best corrected <strong>visual acuity</strong> for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and first or second Class). <strong>If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change.</strong></p>
<p>3. Copies of any testing performed (visual fields, etc.)</p>
<p>4. <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/Quality_of_Vision_Questionnaire.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">Quality of Vision Questionnaire</a></p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance</p>
<p>Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> If unable to meet 1st or 2nd class visual acuity standards, but they will accept a lower class, the AME should issue 3rd class and annotate block 60.</p>"
amblyopia-lazy-eye-status-summary-developmental-adaptation-to-unilateral-visual,Amblyopia (Lazy Eye) Status Summary (Developmental adaptation to unilateral visual impairment*),"C. If cataract surgery has been performed, see Lens Implant Disposition Table.",,,"<p><strong>C.</strong> If cataract surgery has been performed, see <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/Lens_Implant_dispo_table.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">Lens Implant Disposition Table.</a></p>",,
diplopia-double-vision,DIPLOPIA (DOUBLE VISION),A. Any history or current symptoms. Include persistent or intermittent.,"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their treating ophthalmologist, neurologist, or neuro-ophthalmologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include: - A thorough assessment identifying the underlying CAUSE of the diplopia. - Objective evidence of stability or resolution of the misalignment. (e.g. a resolved 6th nerve paresis) - Documentation of effective treatment (e.g., prism, surgical correction), if used. - 3. Visual acuity. The best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate if age 50 or older and 1st or 2nd class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. - 4. Copies of any testing already performed (visual fields, etc.)",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong> Any history or current symptoms.</p>
<p>Include persistent or intermittent.</p>","<p><strong>Submit the following for FAA review: </strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with their treating <strong>ophthalmologist, neurologist, or neuro-ophthalmologist </strong>no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<ul><li><strong>2. It must specifically include: </strong></li><li>A thorough assessment identifying the <strong>underlying CAUSE</strong> of the diplopia.</li><li>Objective evidence of <strong>stability or resolution</strong> of the misalignment. (e.g. a resolved 6th nerve paresis)</li><li><strong>Documentation of effective treatment</strong> (e.g., prism, surgical correction), if used.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>3. Visual acuity</strong>. The best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate if age 50 or older and 1st or 2nd class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>4. </strong>Copies of any testing already performed (visual fields, etc.)</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Summarize findings in Item 60.</p>"
diplopia-double-vision,DIPLOPIA (DOUBLE VISION),B. Due to a neurological condition,"If due to a neurological condition, see corresponding page.",,"<p><strong>B. </strong> Due to a <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/app_process/exam_tech/item46/amd"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">neurological condition</a></p>","<p>If due to a <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/app_process/exam_tech/item46/amd"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">neurological condition,</a> see corresponding page.</p>",
diplopia-double-vision,DIPLOPIA (DOUBLE VISION),A. Any history of ocular histoplasmosis (single or multiple episodes; resolved or currently being treated),"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating ophthalmologist or retinal specialist (preferred) no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment and plan; - Prognosis; and Follow-up. 2. It must specifically include: - Clinical correlation of any findings including if treatment is indicated; - A highly descriptive retinal fundus exam (including periphery) with retinal drawing; - Specific comment whether the condition is static or progressive; and - OCT (optical coherence test) with statement if any neovascularization 3. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include the best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and first or second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. 4. Visual field testing (30-2) of both eyes performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - If any macular involvement, central visual field testing (10-2 or 12-2) is also required. 5. Copies of any other testing already performed.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.,"<p><strong>A.</strong> Any history of ocular histoplasmosis</p>
<p>(single or multiple episodes;</p>
<p>resolved or currently being treated)</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li>1. A <strong>current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</strong> generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>ophthalmologist or retinal specialist (preferred) </strong>no more than <strong>90 days</strong> prior to the AME exam. It must include:</li></ul>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment and plan;</li><li>Prognosis; and Follow-up.</li></ul>
<p>2. It must specifically include:</p>
<ul><li>Clinical correlation of any findings including if treatment is indicated;</li><li>A highly descriptive retinal fundus exam (including periphery) with retinal drawing;</li><li>Specific comment whether the condition is static or progressive; and</li><li>OCT (optical coherence test) with statement if any neovascularization</li></ul>
<p>3. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include the best corrected <strong>visual acuity</strong> for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and first or second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change.</p>
<p>4. Visual field testing <strong>(30-2) of both eyes</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</p>
<ul><li>If any macular involvement, central visual field testing (10-2 or 12-2) is also required.</li></ul>
<p>5. Copies of any other testing already performed.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance</p>
<p>Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.</p>"
diplopia-double-vision,DIPLOPIA (DOUBLE VISION),"Congenital or acquired conditions (whether acute or chronic) of either eye or adnexa, that may interfere with visual functions, may progress to that degree, or may be aggravated by flying (tumors and ptosis obscuring the pupil, acute inflammatory disease of the eyes and lids, or keratoconus.)","Provide completed FAA Form 8500-7 Submit all pertinent medical information and current status report For keratoconus, include if available results of imaging studies such as kertatometry, videokeratography, etc., with clinical correlation Note: applicant should be at best corrected visual acuity before evaluation",Requires FAA Decision,"<p>Congenital or acquired conditions (whether acute or chronic) of either eye or adnexa, that may interfere with visual functions, may progress to that degree, or may be aggravated by flying (tumors and ptosis obscuring the pupil, acute inflammatory disease of the eyes and lids, or keratoconus.)</p>","<p>Provide completed FAA Form 8500-7</p>
