chorioretinitis,CHORIORETINITIS,B. All others - Treatment in previous 5 years - Multiple episodes - Likely to recur - Does not meet VA specifications,"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with treating retinal specialist (preferred) or ophthalmologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include: - Diagnosis & Etiology: Specific cause/etiology and presence of any underlying immunodeficiency. - Date treatment was completed and verification that the condition is fully resolved with no active disease. - Prognosis and confirmation of no current visual impairment. - 3. Visual Acuity. Best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and First or Second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. - 4. Visual field testing (24-2 or 30-2) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 5. Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) testing performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.","DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60. Note: If unable to meet visual acuity or a visual field defect remains, a MFT may be required. Annotate which FSDO the airman wants to use.","<p><strong>B. </strong></p>
<p>All others</p>
<ul><li>Treatment in previous 5 years</li></ul>
<ul><li>Multiple episodes</li></ul>
<ul><li>Likely to recur</li></ul>
<ul><li>Does not meet VA specifications</li></ul>","<p><strong>Submit the following for FAA review: </strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with treating <strong>retinal specialist (preferred) or ophthalmologist </strong>no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<ul><li><strong>2. It must specifically include:</strong></li></ul>
<ul><li>Diagnosis &amp; Etiology: Specific cause/etiology and presence of any underlying immunodeficiency.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Date treatment was completed and verification that the condition is fully resolved with no active disease.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Prognosis and confirmation of no current visual impairment.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>3. Visual Acuity.</strong> Best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and First or Second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>4. Visual field testing</strong> (24-2 or 30-2) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>5. Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) testing</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Summarize findings in Item 60.</p>
<p><strong>Note: </strong>If unable to meet visual acuity or a visual field defect remains, a MFT may be required. Annotate which FSDO the airman wants to use.</p>"
