chorioretinitis,CHORIORETINITIS,A. Any history of optic neuritis (single or multiple episodes; resolved or currently being treated),"Submit the following for FAA review: - 3. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating ophthalmologist or retinal specialist (preferred) no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; - Assessment and plan; - Prognosis; and Follow-up. 2. It must specifically include: - Date treatment completed or if any ongoing treatment or surveillance recommended; - If the condition has resolved; - Underlying cause if identified; - Is the presentation consistent with typical or atypical; - Are there any symptoms, current problems, or remaining abnormalities which could interfere with flight or ATCS duties 3. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include the best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and First or Second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. 4. Visual field testing (30-2 or 24-2) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. 5. MRI of the brain and orbits with and without contrast for demyelinating disease. 6. A current, detailed neurological evaluation that meets FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation generated from a clinic visit with the treating neurologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. - 7. Color vision testing must be performed for each eye separately using an FAA acceptable computer-based color vision test, within 90 days prior to the AME exam. Pay particular attention to red desaturation. One-time screening is not sufficient; repeated testing is required, as optic neuritis can affect color vision over time. - 8. Copies of any testing performed (visual fields, etc.)",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.,"<p><strong>A.</strong> Any history of optic neuritis</p>
<p>(single or multiple episodes;</p>
<p>resolved or currently being treated)</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li>3. A <strong>current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</strong> generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>ophthalmologist or retinal specialist (preferred) </strong>no more than</li></ul>
<p><strong>90 days </strong>prior to the AME exam. It must include:</p>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>Results of any testing performed;</li><li>Diagnosis;</li><li>Assessment and plan;</li><li>Prognosis; and Follow-up.</li></ul>
<p>2. It must specifically include:</p>
<ul><li>Date treatment completed or if any ongoing treatment or surveillance recommended;</li><li>If the condition has resolved;</li><li>Underlying cause if identified;</li><li>Is the presentation consistent with typical or atypical;</li><li>Are there any symptoms, current problems, or remaining abnormalities which could interfere with flight or ATCS duties</li></ul>
<p>3. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include the best corrected <strong>visual acuity</strong> for distant and near vision (intermediate - if age 50 or older and First or Second Class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change.</p>
<p>4. Visual field testing (30-2 or 24-2) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</p>
<p>5. MRI of the brain and orbits with and without contrast for demyelinating disease.</p>
<p>6. A current, detailed neurological evaluation that meets <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/NeurologicalSpecificationSheet.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener""><strong>FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation</strong></a> generated from a</p>
<p>clinic visit with the treating neurologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</p>
<ul><li>7. <strong>Color vision testing </strong>must be performed for each eye separately using an FAA acceptable computer-based color vision test, within 90 days prior to the AME exam. Pay particular attention to red desaturation. One-time screening is not sufficient; repeated testing is required, as optic neuritis can affect color vision over time.</li></ul>
<ul><li>8. Copies of any testing performed (visual fields, etc.)</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance</p>
<p>Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.</p>"
chorioretinitis,CHORIORETINITIS,"A. Any history of Note: In most cases, retinal dystrophies (e.g. Cone, Rod, Rod- Cone, Cone-Rod, Retinitis Pigmentosa) are incompatible with medical certification/clearance.","Submit the following for FAA review: 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note* generated from a clinic visit with a board-certified Retinal Specialist (subspecialty of ophthalmology), no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. An FAA 8500-7 or 8500-14 are NOT acceptable. The progress note must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - results of any testing performed; - diagnosis; - assessment and plan; - prognosis; and follow-up. 2. It must specifically include: - The eye specialist’s interpretation of any testing performed. - Whether the dystrophy is associated with any medical syndrome. - Any treatments. 3. Copies of the most recent testing already performed for this condition such as: - Reliable threshold visual fields. - Electroretinography (ERG); - Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT); - Fundus imaging and autofluorescence; - Any other testing already performed. 4. Visual acuity testing no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. *NOTE: Submission of 8500-7 or 8500-14 instead of actual clinical progress notes is NOT acceptable.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA.,"<p>A. Any history of</p>
<p>Note: In most cases, retinal dystrophies (e.g. Cone, Rod, Rod- Cone, Cone-Rod, Retinitis Pigmentosa) are incompatible with medical certification/clearance.</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p>1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note* generated from a clinic visit with a <strong>board-certified Retinal Specialist</strong> (subspecialty of ophthalmology), no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. An FAA 8500-7 or 8500-14 are NOT acceptable. The progress note must include:</p>
<ul><li>A detailed summary of the history of the condition;</li><li>Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any);</li><li>Physical exam findings;</li><li>results of any testing performed;</li><li>diagnosis;</li><li>assessment and plan;</li><li>prognosis; and follow-up.</li></ul>
<p>2. It must specifically include:</p>
<ul><li>The eye specialist’s interpretation of any testing performed.</li><li>Whether the dystrophy is associated with any medical syndrome.</li><li>Any treatments.</li></ul>
<p>3. Copies of the most recent <strong>testing already performed</strong> for this condition such as:</p>
<ul><li>Reliable threshold visual fields.</li><li>Electroretinography (ERG);</li><li>Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT);</li><li>Fundus imaging and autofluorescence;</li><li>Any other testing already performed.</li></ul>
<p>4. Visual acuity testing no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</p>
<p>*NOTE: Submission of 8500-7 or 8500-14 instead of actual clinical progress notes is <strong>NOT</strong> acceptable.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA.</p>"
retinoschisis,RETINOSCHISIS,A. Any history (Reported by the individual or seen on progress notes.),"Submit the following for FAA review: - 5. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their treating retina specialist (ophthalmologist) no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 6. Visual acuity: The best corrected visual acuity for distant and near vision (intermediate if age 50 or older and 1st or 2nd class). If vision standards are not met, that should be discussed. Include if the visual acuity is expected to change. - 7. Visual field testing (30-2) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 8. Copies of any testing already performed such as electroretinogram (ERG) and optical coherence tomography (OCT).",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong>Any history</p>
