wolff-parkinson-white-wpw-and-other-pre-excitation-conditions,WOLFF PARKINSON WHITE (WPW) and other pre-excitation conditions,A. WPW PATTERN on EKG/ECG - Incidental finding on EKG/ECG - NO symptoms - Never required treatment,"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their treating electrophysiologist or cardiologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include if the diagnosis is WPW pattern, WPW syndrome, or other. - 3. Echocardiogram (Echo). Report from a 2-D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 4. 24-hour cardiac monitor performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. ≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should have atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses > 2.0 or 2.5 seconds; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter. - 5. Exercise stress test (EST) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 6. Other testing. Copies of any testing deemed clinically necessary by the treating physician.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong>WPW <strong>PATTERN</strong> on EKG/ECG</p>
<ul><li>Incidental finding on EKG/ECG</li><li>NO symptoms</li><li>Never required treatment</li></ul>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with their treating <strong>electrophysiologist</strong> or <strong>cardiologist</strong> no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<ul><li><strong>2. It must specifically include</strong> if the diagnosis is WPW pattern, WPW syndrome, or other.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>3. Echocardiogram (Echo).</strong> Report from a 2-D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>4. 24-hour cardiac monitor</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should have atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses &gt; 2.0 or 2.5 seconds; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter.</p>
<ul><li><strong>5. Exercise stress test (EST)</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>6. Other testing. </strong>Copies of any testing deemed clinically necessary by the treating physician.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Summarize findings in Item 60.</p>"
wolff-parkinson-white-wpw-and-other-pre-excitation-conditions,WOLFF PARKINSON WHITE (WPW) and other pre-excitation conditions,B. WPW SYNDROME TREATED with radiofrequency ablation - ANY time in the past AND - Does not need antiarrhythmic medication to sustain Note: Do NOT use the typical atrial flutter page.,"After a 3-month recovery, obtain the following and submit for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their treating electrophysiologist or cardiologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include: - Date(s) of ablation procedure(s); - if anti-arrhythmia medications are needed; and - any continued symptoms. - 3. Echocardiogram (Echo). Report from a 2- D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 4. 24-hour cardiac monitor performed at least 90 days AFTER THE ABLATION and no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. ≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should have atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses > 2.0 or 2.5 seconds; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter. - 5. Exercise stress test (EST) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 6. Other testing. Copies of any testing deemed clinically necessary by the treating physician. - 7. Copies of all ablation procedure reports.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60.,"<p><strong>B.</strong> WPW <strong>SYNDROME TREATED </strong></p>
<p><strong>with radiofrequency ablation </strong></p>
<ul><li>ANY time in the past</li></ul>
<p>AND</p>
<ul><li>Does not need antiarrhythmic medication to sustain</li></ul>
<p>Note: <strong>Do NOT use the typical atrial flutter page. </strong></p>","<p>After a <strong>3-month recovery,</strong> obtain the following and submit for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with their treating <strong>electrophysiologist</strong> or <strong>cardiologist</strong> no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<ul><li><strong>2. It must specifically include:</strong></li><li>Date(s) of ablation procedure(s);</li><li>if anti-arrhythmia medications are needed; and</li><li>any continued symptoms.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>3. Echocardiogram (Echo).</strong> Report from a 2- D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>4. 24-hour cardiac monitor</strong> performed <strong>at least 90 days AFTER THE ABLATION and </strong>no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should have atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses &gt; 2.0 or 2.5 seconds; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter.</p>
<ul><li><strong>5. Exercise stress test (EST)</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>6. Other testing. </strong>Copies of any testing deemed clinically necessary by the treating physician.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>7. </strong>Copies of all ablation procedure reports.</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Summarize findings in Item 60.</p>"
