heart-transplant-cardiac-transplant-heart-replacement,"HEART TRANSPLANT (cardiac transplant, heart replacement)",A. 1st or 2nd class and ATCS,Submit the following for FAA review: The most recent detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their cardiologist or cardiac surgeon which verifies the transplant has been performed.,DEFER Submit the information to the FAA verifying the procedure and reason it was performed.,<p><strong>A. 1st or 2nd class and ATCS</strong></p>,"<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<p>The most recent <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with their <strong>cardiologist or cardiac surgeon </strong>which verifies the transplant has been performed.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA verifying the procedure and reason it was performed.</p>"
heart-transplant-cardiac-transplant-heart-replacement,"HEART TRANSPLANT (cardiac transplant, heart replacement)",B. 3rd class only,"There is NO mandatory recovery period per Public Law 114-190 May 2017, but should be released by the treating physician. After the treating cardiologist and/or transplant surgeon has determined that the condition is stable, obtain the following evaluation(s) and submit them to the FAA for review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating cardiologist or transplant surgeon no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. - 2. It must specifically include: - A statement regarding functional capacity, modifiable cardiovascular risk factors, and prognosis for incapacitation; - Any evidence of rejection and complete documentation of all rejection history, whether treated or not; - A complete history regarding any infectious process; - A complete history regarding any malignancy; and - Comment on the donor heart status, including any evidence of allograft vasculopathy or any disease state. - 3. Labs performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam: - Hemoglobin A1C and - Lipid profile to include total cholesterol, HDL, LDL, and triglycerides. - 4. Hospital records to include: - Operative report; - Discharge summary from the physician (NOT the discharge instructions/paperwork given to the patient when leaving the hospital); - Admission (history & physical); - Pathology report (if performed); and - DO NOT submit miscellaneous hospital records such as flowsheets, nursing notes, physician orders, and medication administration records. - 5. Coronary Angiogram [cardiac cath] performed no more than 12 months before the AME exam: - Must submit both of the report(s); and - Actual imaging on CD in DICOM format. - 6. 24-hour cardiac monitor performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam: ≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should include atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses 2.5 seconds or greater; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and the percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter. - 7. Stress Echo. Graded Exercise Stress ECHO (with electrical tracings) no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam in accordance with the enclosed specification sheet. - 8. Any other testing already completed or deemed necessary by the treating physician (e.g., myocardial biopsy)","DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Once all information is received, these cases are reviewed by a FAS Cardiothoracic Surgery consultant.",<p><strong>B. 3rd class only</strong></p>,"<p>There is NO mandatory recovery period per Public Law 114-190 May 2017, but should be released by the treating physician.</p>
<p>After the treating cardiologist and/or transplant surgeon has determined that the condition is stable, obtain the following evaluation(s) and submit them to the FAA for review:</p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with the treating <strong>cardiologist or transplant surgeon</strong> no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<ul><li><p><strong>2. </strong>It must specifically include:</p>
<ul><li>A statement regarding functional capacity, modifiable cardiovascular risk factors, and prognosis for incapacitation;</li><li>Any evidence of rejection and complete documentation of all rejection history, whether treated or not;</li><li>A complete history regarding any infectious process;</li><li>A complete history regarding any malignancy; and</li><li>Comment on the donor heart status, including any evidence of allograft vasculopathy or any disease state.</li></ul></li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>3. Labs </strong>performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam:</li><li>Hemoglobin A1C and</li><li>Lipid profile to include total cholesterol, HDL, LDL, and triglycerides.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>4. </strong>Hospital records to include:</li><li><strong>Operative report;</strong></li><li><strong>Discharge summary from the physician</strong> (NOT the discharge instructions/paperwork given to the patient when leaving the hospital);</li><li>Admission (history &amp; physical);</li><li>Pathology report (if performed); and</li><li><strong>DO NOT submit </strong>miscellaneous hospital records such as flowsheets, nursing notes, physician orders, and medication administration records.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>5. Coronary Angiogram [cardiac cath]</strong> performed no more than 12 months before the AME exam:</li><li>Must submit both of the report(s); and</li><li>Actual imaging on CD in DICOM format.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>6. 24-hour cardiac monitor</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam:</li></ul>
<p>≥ 24-hour cardiac monitor (such as a Holter, Zio Patch, etc.). It should include atrial and ventricular ectopic counts; hourly tabular data to include the longest pause duration and counts of all pauses 2.5 seconds or greater; heart rate (max, min, and average); other histograms and frequency graphs; and the percentage of atrial fibrillation/flutter.</p>
<ul><li><strong>7. Stress Echo</strong>. Graded Exercise Stress ECHO (with electrical tracings) no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam in accordance with the enclosed specification sheet.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>8. </strong>Any other testing already completed or deemed necessary by the treating physician (e.g., myocardial biopsy)</li></ul>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Once all information is received, these cases are reviewed by a FAS Cardiothoracic Surgery consultant.</p>"
hypertrophic-cardiomyopathy-hcm-hypertrophic-obstructive-cardiomyopathy-hocm-or,"Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM), Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (HOCM), or Idiopathic Hypertrophic Subaortic Stenosis (IHSS)",A. Any history or suspected history (all classes),"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with their treating cardiologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up. It MUST include - - a. Complete history detailing any family history of unexplained sudden cardiac death, and - b. Any personal history of non-sustained Ventricular Tachycardia, or unexplained syncope. Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy 5-Year SCD Score: Documentation of a 5-year sudden cardiac death risk score (compiled using the most recent version of the American Heart Association (AHA) risk calculator). - 2. Echocardiogram (Echo) performed WITH Valsalva maneuver. Report and digital images from a 2-D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. Must include complete measurements and specific attention to maximal LV wall thickness, left atrial size, and maximal LV outflow tract gradient (rest vs. Valsalva). Submit the video resulting from this study on CD- ROM in DICOM compatible format. - 3. Cardiac monitor. 72 hours minimum performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. Note: Some cases may require a 30-day monitor. The report must include the data summary and representative EKG tracings. - 4. Maximal Exercise stress test (EST) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. Submit the report with worksheet and representative EKG tracings (to exclude CAD). STRESS TEST REQUIREMENTS - Protocol: 3-minute Bruce treadmill test. Must complete Stage 3 (>= 9 minutes) and reach 100% predicted maximal heart rate unless medically contraindicated or prevented by symptoms or medication. - Medication: Stop beta-blockers, calcium blockers (Diltiazem/Verapamil), and digoxin for 48 hours before the test. If it is unsafe to stop, attach a doctor's note describing the contraindication. - Treadmill Not Possible: Provide a doctor's note to explain why and perform either bicycle ergometry (Must reach 100% max Heart Rate and greater than METS) or a Nuclear Stress Test (pharmacological stress radionuclide myocardial perfusion scintigraphy). - ECG Printouts Required: Full 12-lead taken at rest (supine and standing). 12 lead tracings + rhythm strips during each stage, at the end of each stage, at peak, and every minute during recovery for at least 5-minutes or until the tracings return to baseline level. Computer generated, sample-cycle ECG tracings or summary sheets are not acceptable. - Submit original printouts only (by mail or electronic upload). Faxes or photocopies are not accepted. - 5. EKG (resting) performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. - 6. Cardiac MRI performed no more than 12 months prior to the AME exam. Include report and actual digital images. - 7. Historical Medical Records - Past Cardiac Care: Submit all prior heart evaluation and treatment records. Include records for any procedures (e.g., myomectomy, alcohol ablation, chordal realignment). - Operative Reports: Submit official operative reports for all past heart surgeries. (If applicable) - Advanced Imaging & Tests: Submit all prior test results and advanced imaging (e.g., Chest CT, MRI). Provide both the written reports AND the actual image files. Note: once these records are reviewed, additional testing or information may be required.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance. Summarize findings in Item 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong></p>
<p>Any history or suspected history</p>
<p>(all classes)</p>","<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li><strong>1. </strong>A <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/PilotInformation_DetailedClinicalProgressNote.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">current, detailed Clinical Progress Note</a> generated from a clinic visit with their treating <strong>cardiologist</strong> no more than 90 days before the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>It should include a summary of the history of the condition or diagnosis; current medications, dosages, and side effects (if any); clinical exam findings; results of any testing performed; diagnosis; assessment and plan (prognosis); and follow-up.</p>
<p>It <strong>MUST</strong> include</p>
<ul><li><ul><li>a. Complete history detailing any family history of unexplained sudden cardiac death, and</li><li>b. Any personal history of non-sustained Ventricular Tachycardia, or unexplained syncope.</li></ul></li></ul>
<p><strong>Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy 5-Year SCD Score:</strong> Documentation of a 5-year sudden cardiac death risk score (compiled using the most recent version of the American Heart Association (AHA) risk calculator).</p>
<ul><li><strong>2. Echocardiogram</strong> (Echo) performed WITH Valsalva maneuver. Report and digital images from a 2-D and Doppler flow study performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. Must include complete measurements and specific attention to maximal LV wall thickness, left atrial size, and maximal LV outflow tract gradient (rest vs. Valsalva).</li></ul>
<p>Submit the video resulting from this study on CD- ROM in DICOM compatible format.</p>
<ul><li><strong>3. Cardiac monitor. 72 hours minimum</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. Note: Some cases may require a 30-day monitor.</li></ul>
<p>The report must include the data summary and representative EKG tracings.</p>
<ul><li><strong>4. Maximal Exercise stress test (EST)</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p>Submit the report with worksheet and representative EKG tracings (to exclude CAD).</p>
<p><strong>STRESS TEST REQUIREMENTS</strong></p>
<ul><li><strong>Protocol:</strong> 3-minute Bruce treadmill test. Must complete Stage 3 (&gt;= 9 minutes) and reach 100% predicted maximal heart rate unless medically contraindicated or prevented by symptoms or medication.</li><li><strong>Medication:</strong> Stop beta-blockers, calcium blockers (Diltiazem/Verapamil), and digoxin for 48 hours before the test. If it is unsafe to stop, attach a doctor&#x27;s note describing the contraindication.</li><li><strong>Treadmill Not Possible: </strong>Provide a doctor&#x27;s note to explain why and perform either bicycle ergometry (Must reach 100% max Heart Rate and greater than METS) or a Nuclear Stress Test (pharmacological stress radionuclide myocardial perfusion scintigraphy).</li><li><strong>ECG Printouts Required:</strong> Full 12-lead taken at rest (supine and standing). 12 lead tracings + rhythm strips during each stage, at the end of each stage, at peak, and every minute during recovery for at least 5-minutes or until the tracings return to baseline level. Computer generated, sample-cycle ECG tracings or summary sheets are not acceptable.</li><li><strong>Submit</strong> original printouts only (by mail or electronic upload). Faxes or photocopies are not accepted.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>5. EKG (resting)</strong> performed no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>6. Cardiac MRI</strong> performed no more than <strong>12 months prior</strong> to the AME exam. Include report and actual digital images.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>7. Historical Medical Records </strong></li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Past Cardiac Care:</strong> Submit all prior heart evaluation and treatment records. Include records for any procedures (e.g., myomectomy, alcohol ablation, chordal realignment).</li><li><strong>Operative Reports:</strong> Submit official operative reports for all past heart surgeries. (If applicable)</li><li><strong>Advanced Imaging &amp; Tests:</strong> Submit all prior test results and advanced imaging (e.g., Chest CT, MRI). <strong>Provide both the written reports AND the actual image files.</strong></li></ul>
<p><strong>Note: </strong> once these records are reviewed, additional testing or information may be required.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>
<p>Summarize findings in Item 60.</p>"
