eosinophilic-esophagitis,Eosinophilic Esophagitis,"A. Small varices (grade 1 or F1) without red signs If the underlying cause is identified (e.g., cirrhosis ), see that page.","Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed Clinical Progress Note generated from a clinic visit with a board-certified Gastroenterologist, no more than 90 days prior to the AME exam. It must include: - A detailed summary of the history of the condition; - Current medications, dosage, and side effects (if any); - Physical exam findings; - Results of any testing performed; - Diagnosis; assessment and plan; - Prognosis; and follow-up. - 2. The Clinical Progress Note must specifically include: - Underlying cause of the varices; - Dates of any bleeding events, treatment, and outcome; - If no history of bleeding events, that should be stated; - Size, grade, and location of varices; - ANY red signs (e.g., red wale markings, erythematous raised spots, also referred to as cherry red spots); and - Prognosis for risk of bleeding. - 3. Report from the most recent upper endoscopy study/EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) - 4. Labs: - Complete Blood Count (CBC) performed within the past 90 days; and - Copies of other labs already performed or clinically indicated. - 5. Results of any other testing already performed or clinically indicated.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance Annotate (elements or findings) in Item 60.,"<p><strong>A. </strong></p>
<p>Small varices (grade 1 or F1)</p>
<p>without red signs</p>
