arteriovenous-malformation-avm-neurological-brain-or-spinal-cord,ARTERIOVENOUS MALFORMATION (AVM) – Neurological (Brain or Spinal Cord),C. Tourette Syndrome,"Submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed neurological evaluation that meets FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation generated from a clinic visit with the treating neurologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. - 2. A Neuropsychological (NP) evaluation that meets FAA Specifications for Neuropsychological Evaluations for ADHD from a clinic visit with the treating neuropsychologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. Note: Tourette's syndrome and tics are commonly associated with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD). Neuropsychological evaluation and testing is required.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.,<p><strong>C. </strong>Tourette Syndrome</p>,"<p>Submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li>1. A <strong>current, detailed neurological evaluation</strong> that meets <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/NeurologicalSpecificationSheet.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation</a> generated from a clinic visit with the <strong>treating neurologist no more than 90 days before</strong> the AME exam.</li><li>2. A <strong>Neuropsychological (NP) evaluation</strong> that meets FAA <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/dec_cons/disease_prot/adhd"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">Specifications for Neuropsychological Evaluations for ADHD</a> from a clinic visit with the <strong>treating neuropsychologist no more than 90 days before </strong>the AME exam.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Note: </strong>Tourette&#x27;s syndrome and tics are commonly associated with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD). Neuropsychological evaluation and testing is required.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance</p>
<p>Annotate (elements or findings) in Block 60.</p>"
arteriovenous-malformation-avm-neurological-brain-or-spinal-cord,ARTERIOVENOUS MALFORMATION (AVM) – Neurological (Brain or Spinal Cord),A. ULOC Unexplained Loss of Consciousness current or historical,"After a 2-year, symptom-free recovery period, obtain and submit the following for FAA review: - 1. A current, detailed neurological evaluation that meets FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation generated from a clinic visit with the treating neurologist no more than 90 days before the AME exam. - 2. The neurologic evaluation must specifically include: - A description of the event(s) and a summary of all testing or evaluation(s) performed to identify the cause; - If there has been any recurrence and the length of time without symptoms; and - If no cause was identified, that should be stated. - 3. Copies of any testing already performed such as lab, imaging, EEG, or other testing. - 4. Hospital or clinic records from this episode and follow-up. This may include History and Physical (H&P), operative notes, and hospital discharge summary (if applicable). If no neurological explanation is found, additional evaluations may be required on a case-by-case basis such as: - Cardiovascular evaluation from a cardiologist; - Exercise stress test; - Holter monitor; and/or - TTE Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) - If the above items have already been performed, they should be submitted to the FAA. If not yet performed, the applicant may want - to wait until FAA review of their case before undergoing additional testing. Note: Upon review of the above items, additional information or testing may be required. If a cause is identified - see that section.",DEFER Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.,"<p><strong>A.</strong> ULOC</p>
<p>Unexplained Loss of Consciousness</p>
<p>current or historical</p>","<p>After a <strong>2-year, symptom-free recovery period</strong>, obtain and submit the following for FAA review:</p>
<ul><li>1. A <strong>current, detailed neurological evaluation</strong> that meets <a href=""https://www.faa.gov/ame_guide/media/NeurologicalSpecificationSheet.pdf"" target=""_blank"" rel=""noopener"">FAA Specifications for Neurologic Evaluation</a> generated from a clinic visit with the<strong> treating neurologist no more than 90 days before</strong> the AME exam.</li></ul>
<ul><li>2. The neurologic evaluation must specifically include:</li><li>A description of the event(s) and a summary of all testing or evaluation(s) performed to identify the cause;</li><li>If there has been any recurrence and the length of time without symptoms; and</li><li>If no cause was identified, that should be stated.</li></ul>
<ul><li>3. Copies of any testing already performed such as lab, imaging, EEG, or other testing.</li></ul>
<ul><li>4. Hospital or clinic records from this episode and follow-up. This may include History and Physical (H&amp;P), operative notes, and hospital discharge summary (if applicable).</li></ul>
<p><strong>If no neurological explanation is found</strong>, additional evaluations may be required on a case-by-case basis such as:</p>
<ul><li>Cardiovascular evaluation from a cardiologist;</li><li>Exercise stress test;</li><li>Holter monitor; and/or</li><li>TTE Transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE)</li><li>If the above items have already been performed, they should be submitted to the FAA. If not yet performed, the applicant may want</li></ul>
<ul><li>to wait until FAA review of their case before undergoing additional testing.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Note: </strong>Upon review of the above items, additional information or testing may be required.</p>
<p>If a cause is identified - see that section.</p>","<p><strong>DEFER </strong></p>
<p>Submit the information to the FAA for a possible Special Issuance.</p>"
